---
import PrivacyCrisisRegions from "@/components/privacy/PrivacyCrisisRegions.astro";
import { FREE_PLAN_SESSION_LIMIT } from "@/lib/session-data/quota";
import { isPeopleMemoryEnabled } from "@/lib/session-flow/people-memory-mode";
import { isTopicMapEnabled } from "@/lib/session-flow/topic-map-mode";
import { getSupportContact } from "@/lib/support-contact";

/**
 * English content of the “Privacy and terms” page. Legal prose with links in
 * the middle of sentences lives in one partial per language; section ids are
 * neutral and identical in both partials (a test keeps them in sync).
 */
const support = getSupportContact();
// People cards are described only while the feature ships; the same flag hides
// the dashboard section and the extraction, so the page never promises more.
const peopleMemory = isPeopleMemoryEnabled();
// The topic map follows the same rule under its own flag.
const topicMap = isTopicMapEnabled();
---

<section id="what-is" class="scroll-mt-20">
  <h2 class="text-ink text-xl font-semibold">What SafeSpace is</h2>
  <p class="text-ink-soft mt-3">
    SafeSpace is an educational conversation simulation supported by AI. It shows what work in different psychotherapy
    approaches can look like and helps you sort out your thoughts before a real conversation with a professional.
  </p>
  <ul class="text-ink-soft mt-3 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>It is not therapy, a diagnosis or medical advice.</li>
    <li>It does not replace contact with a psychotherapist, a doctor or crisis help.</li>
    <li>It does not choose a therapy approach for you — the choice of perspective is educational.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="data" class="scroll-mt-20">
  <h2 class="text-ink text-xl font-semibold">What data we process</h2>
  <dl class="mt-3 space-y-4">
    <div>
      <dt class="text-ink font-semibold">Account</dt>
      <dd class="text-ink-soft mt-1">
        Your e‑mail address and sign-in details (a password or a Google account), plus the chosen interface language,
        which is also stored in a cookie on this device. The account is needed so that conversations and their history
        stay private and visible only to you.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt class="text-ink font-semibold">Conversation content</dt>
      <dd class="text-ink-soft mt-1">
        The messages you write or dictate, the avatar's replies, an automatic summary of the history kept separately for
        each avatar, and optional summaries of individual conversations. A voice recording is used only to transcribe it
        into text and is not stored.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt class="text-ink font-semibold">Usage data</dt>
      <dd class="text-ink-soft mt-1">
        The date, status and length of a conversation, the chosen perspective and technical logs of the service's
        operation. The logs contain no conversation content, e‑mail addresses or full account identifiers.
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>

<section id="who-sees" class="scroll-mt-20">
  <h2 class="text-ink text-xl font-semibold">Who sees your conversations</h2>
  <p class="text-ink-soft mt-3">
    Only you. The content of conversations and summaries is available only after signing in to your account. SafeSpace
    administrators see only aggregate statistics and account data (e.g. the creation date, status, plan) — they have no
    access to conversation content. There is no “back door” to view conversations either; any legal or safety exception
    would require a separate, openly described procedure.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="ai" class="scroll-mt-20">
  <h2 class="text-ink text-xl font-semibold">How AI works in a conversation</h2>
  <p class="text-ink-soft mt-3">
    The avatar's replies are generated by a language model provided by an external provider (OpenRouter). To make that
    possible, the content of the current conversation — the latest messages and the automatic summary of earlier
    conversations with this avatar — is sent to that provider to generate a reply. Every message of yours also goes
    through an automatic safety assessment; if it indicates an urgent danger, the simulation is stopped and we show help
    numbers.
  </p>
  <p class="text-ink-soft mt-3">
    Text requests go only to endpoints that declare no data collection and zero data retention (ZDR). Dictation uses a
    separate OpenRouter transcription interface: the recording is sent only after an active session is confirmed and is
    not stored in SafeSpace; only the transcribed text enters the history, if you send it.
  </p>
  <p class="text-ink-soft mt-3">
    Every new conversation automatically receives a summary of all available earlier conversations with the same avatar.
    While preparing the memory, AI processes the transcripts of those conversations in batches — also in the background
    on the conversation's closing screen and while you are on the dashboard. The new conversation receives a condensed
    summary, not full transcripts. The memory of different avatars is kept separate. A summary may omit details or
    contain errors — you can correct it in the conversation. You can read the current memory on the dashboard. The
    avatar, summaries and memory use the interface language chosen at the time of the conversation; after you change the
    language, earlier transcripts stay in the language they were written in, and the memory may be rewritten in the new
    one.
  </p>
  {
    peopleMemory ? (
      <p class="text-ink-soft mt-3" data-people-memory>
        If you keep “Remember people from conversations” switched on, the avatar also keeps short cards about the people
        you mention: who they are to you, what you said about them, how you feel about it and what you would like to
        change. Every entry comes only from your own words — the avatar does not assess anyone — and shows the
        conversations it came from. You can correct a name or relation, fix or remove an entry, add your own note, or
        ask the avatar to forget a person for good; forgetting also rebuilds the memory of earlier conversations without
        that person, and the name stays on a private list so it is not recorded again. You can switch this off, or
        switch it off and delete all cards, in the account settings. Like the conversation memory, the cards are kept
        separately for each avatar and are produced by the same AI provider under the same zero-retention rules.
      </p>
    ) : null
  }
  {
    topicMap ? (
      <p class="text-ink-soft mt-3" data-topic-map>
        If you keep the “Topic map” switched on, the avatar also notes the difficulties you say you struggle with: how
        they show up, how you cope, how it is now, which people they come up with, and the proposals from conversations,
        your decisions and how they went. Every entry comes only from your own words — a proposal is noted as a
        proposal, never as advice or a plan — and shows the conversations it came from. A difficulty's name carries no
        names of people and no diagnoses; an uncertain link to a person waits for your confirmation. You can rename a
        difficulty, fix or remove an entry, unlink a person, merge two difficulties into one, mark a difficulty as less
        current or remove it entirely. You can switch the map off, or switch it off and delete it entirely, in the
        account settings. The map is kept separately for each avatar and is produced by the same AI provider under the
        same zero-retention rules.
      </p>
    ) : null
  }
</section>

<section id="deletion" class="scroll-mt-20">
  <h2 class="text-ink text-xl font-semibold">Deletion and control</h2>
  <ul class="text-ink-soft mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
    <li>
      To delete a conversation, open its transcript in the “Conversation history” section, choose “Delete conversation”
      and confirm. Deletion erases the content of messages and summaries; only a content-free trace that the
      conversation existed remains — which is why deletion does not restore a used conversation to the allowance.
    </li>
    <li>
      Deleting a conversation also erases this avatar's shared memory and its copies used as context. At the next start
      the memory is rebuilt from the remaining conversations. This does not remove statements already saved in other
      conversations.
    </li>
    {
      peopleMemory ? (
        <li data-people-memory>
          Forgetting a person from their card removes the card, rebuilds the avatar memory without that person and stops
          the avatar from recording that person again. Deleting a conversation also removes every card entry that came
          from it. In the account settings you can delete all people cards at once and switch the feature off.
        </li>
      ) : null
    }
    {
      topicMap ? (
        <li data-topic-map>
          Removing a difficulty from the topic map removes it together with its entries and links to people; deleting a
          conversation removes every map entry that came from it, and forgetting a person removes the entries noted with
          that person. In the account settings you can delete the whole map at once and switch the feature off.
        </li>
      ) : null
    }
    <li>
      You delete the whole account in the account settings: choose “Delete account” and confirm. Deletion covers the
      profile, all conversations, messages, summaries, avatar memory and saved settings. It cannot be undone.
      Administrative events remain without the identifier of the deleted account.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="plans" class="scroll-mt-20">
  <h2 class="text-ink text-xl font-semibold">Free and premium plans</h2>
  <p class="text-ink-soft mt-3">
    The free plan includes {FREE_PLAN_SESSION_LIMIT} time-limited conversations per account (in total, including deleted ones).
    The premium plan has no such limit. At the moment premium is not sold in the app — it is granted by hand by the SafeSpace
    team. If you want to keep talking, contact us.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="crisis" class="scroll-mt-20">
  <h2 class="text-ink text-xl font-semibold">Help in a crisis</h2>
  <p class="text-ink-soft mt-3">
    SafeSpace is not crisis help and does not contact emergency services. If you are in immediate danger, do not wait
    for the app to reply.
  </p>
  <PrivacyCrisisRegions />
</section>

<section id="contact" class="scroll-mt-20">
  <h2 class="text-ink text-xl font-semibold">Contact</h2>
  <p class="text-ink-soft mt-3">
    {
      support.mailtoHref ? (
        <>
          Questions about data, account deletion, access to the premium plan or a blocked account:{" "}
          <a href={support.mailtoHref} class="text-brand font-semibold underline-offset-4 hover:underline">
            {support.email}
          </a>
          .
        </>
      ) : (
        "The SafeSpace team's contact address will be provided here."
      )
    }
  </p>
</section>
